What was the Taconic orogeny and how did it affect the geology of the region between New York and Quebec, Canada?
The Taconic orogeny was a mountain building period that ended 440 million years ago and affected most of modern-day New England. It resulted in the formation of a great mountain range throughout New England and eastern Canada, and perhaps to a lesser degree, southward along the region that is now the Piedmont of eastern North America. 

During this period, the Iapetus Ocean began to close, and the weight of accumulating sediments, in addition to compressional forces in the crust, forced the Iapetus Terrane onto the eastern margin of the continent. This led to the formation of metamorphic rocks such as amphibolite, mica schist, and granites in the region between New York and Quebec, Canada. The newly expanded continental margin gradually stabilized, and the region experienced a tectonically-quiet period until the Late Devonian time.
