The historical context surrounding the publication of 'An Appeal to the Coloured Citizens of the World' is that it was published in 1829 by David Walker, an African-American abolitionist, writer, and anti-slavery activist. This was a response to the growing fear among Southern whites following the Haitian Revolution of 1804 and the Denmark Vesey revolt of 1822, as well as the publication of David Walker's own work, which openly advocated rebellion. The Southern states subsequently implemented anti-literacy laws to control and surveil people of color, as literacy was seen as a threat to their control.
