Identify the Collective for Living Cinema of Lower Manhattan and its significance in the context of the user's question.
The Collective for Living Cinema was an outpost of avant-garde cinema located on White Street in Lower Manhattan in the United States of America. It was an artist-run cooperative that served as an exhibition venue, center for production, and a hub for discourse. The collective presented work by filmmakers outside of the commercial mainstream, including Ken Jacobs, Johan van der Keuken, Yvonne Rainer, and Dziga Vertov, and published scholarly journals on film. It also provided a platform for emerging filmmakers and artists, such as Lizzie Borden, who taught film editing at the Collective. The Collective for Living Cinema was significant in that it created a contemporary and fertile context for avant-garde cinema, allowing artists to overcome the economic, social, and political burdens of the art world.
