What was Operation Buster–Jangle, and what was its significance in military history?
Operation Buster–Jangle was a series of seven nuclear weapons tests conducted by the United States in late 1951 at the Nevada Test Site. It was the first joint test program between the DOD (Operation "Buster") and Los Alamos National Laboratories (Operation "Jangle"). The operation involved 6,500 troops in the Operation Desert Rock I, II, and III exercises, and evaluated the effects of low-yield nuclear devices, including cratering effects.

The significance of Operation Buster–Jangle lies in its role as a pioneering joint test program between the military and scientific communities, marking a crucial step in the development of nuclear warfare capabilities. It also highlighted the need for further research on the residual effects of radiation exposure, as military personnel and civilian observers were in close proximity to the bomb blasts.
